## Release version (Confluence)

Before creating the branch, resolve the **target fix version** from the Production Release Plan:

**Source:** [Production Release Plan](https://vendoroo-dev.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/VA/pages/335511568/Production+Release+Plan)

**How to find the version:**

1. Read the **Release Changelog** section (top entries first).
2. Each block is headed `### Fix Version: vX.Y.Z` with a **Status** line.
3. **Released** versions show `🟢 Released` or `Status: 🟢 Released`.
4. The **target version** is the **latest fix version that is not released yet** — typically `🟡 Pending` at the top of the changelog.

**Example (as of May 2026):** `v6.5.2` is Released; `v6.5.3` is Pending → target is **`v6.5.3`**.

**Mandatory confirmation — release version:**

Always ask the user to confirm the fix version with **`AskQuestion`**, even when you found it confidently.

- Show the version you inferred and the Confluence status (e.g. `v6.5.3 — 🟡 Pending`).
- Options must include the inferred version **and** an **`other`** option so the user can type a different version if lookup failed or the doc is stale.
- If lookup fails or is ambiguous, say so briefly and rely on **`other`** for the user to supply the version.

---

## Release branch name

Pattern: **`release-<version>`**

- Normalize the Confluence fix version by **dropping a leading `v`** for the branch suffix (Confluence `v6.5.3` → branch **`release-6.5.3`**). This matches [`scripts/reset-staging.sh`](scripts/reset-staging.sh) examples.
- **Mandatory confirmation — branch name:** use **`AskQuestion`** to confirm the full branch name (e.g. `release-6.5.3`) before `git checkout -B` / push. Include **`other`** if the user may want a different name.

---

## Target branch (merge destination)

**Merge destination: `production`**

- The release PR **targets `production`** — this is where the release branch merges.
- **Mandatory confirmation — target branch:** use **`AskQuestion`** to confirm `--base production` before `gh pr create`. Default option is **`production`**; include **`other`** only if the user might target a different branch (unusual).

---

## Command intent confirmation (mandatory before running commands)

During release execution, do not run shell commands silently.

- Before each release command batch, explain in plain language **what you are about to run and why**.
- Then ask for confirmation with **`AskQuestion`** (for example: **Run now** / **Edit command** / **Cancel**).
- Wait for user confirmation before running that command batch.
- Apply this to fetch, commit lookup, branch/reset/cherry-pick, verify, push, and PR creation commands.

## Git steps

Before every numbered step below that runs shell commands, perform the **Command intent confirmation** workflow above.

1. **Collect candidate tasks from the user (mandatory):**

   - Use **`AskQuestion`** and ask for Jira keys in one line, e.g. `VAP-1267 VAP-1345`.
   - Confirm the parsed list before continuing.

2. **Confirm origin branch** — see [Origin branch — commit source](#origin-branch--commit-source-release-pr--hotfix-pr). Record **`<origin-branch>`**.

3. **Fetch latest remotes (mandatory before commit lookup):**

   ```bash
   git fetch origin <origin-branch> production
   ```

   Do **not** list commits, review diffs, or create branches until **`origin/<origin-branch>`** and **`origin/production`** are up to date.

4. **Resolve fix version** (Confluence) and get user confirmation (see above).

5. **Confirm branch name** (`release-<version>`) and **merge destination** (`production`) via question boxes.

6. **List commits per ticket** on **`origin/<origin-branch>`** (grep subject/body for `VAP-NNNN`). Prefer the **squash merge** commit for each merged PR. If more than one commit exists for one ticket, include **all** of them for that ticket, in chronological order among themselves.

7. **Order cherry-picks** by **`<origin-branch>` history** (oldest merge first among the selected commits), especially when the same files change across tickets.

8. **Clean working tree** before branch work — stash only if dirty; see [Stash restore (mandatory)](#stash-restore-mandatory).

9. **Create release branch from `<origin-branch>` and scope commits:**

   ```bash
   git fetch origin <origin-branch> production
   git checkout -B release-<version> origin/<origin-branch>
   git reset --hard origin/production
   git cherry-pick <commit1> [<commit2> ...]
   ```

   - **`release-<version>` is created from `origin/<origin-branch>`** (after fetch).
   - **`git reset --hard origin/production`** keeps the release scoped when `<origin-branch>` has unrelated commits. Omit reset + cherry-pick only when the **entire** `origin/<origin-branch>` delta is intended for this release (rare — confirm with user).

10. **Verify scope:** `git log origin/production..HEAD --oneline` — intended release commits plus any dependency commits added to resolve cherry-pick conflicts. Use `git show --stat` per commit locally if something looks off; **do not** paste commit hashes or cherry-pick mechanics into the PR unless a reviewer asked.

   - If cherry-pick fails because a release ticket depends on other work on **`origin/<origin-branch>`**, cherry-pick those **dependency commits** in **`<origin-branch>` order** (oldest first), then continue the release commits. Record every dependency ticket for the PR body (see **⚠️ Included via dependency (cherry-pick)** subsection under Description below).

11. **Push release branch to origin:**

   ```bash
   git push -u origin release-<version>
   ```

12. **Final approval before remote PR (mandatory — wait for user):**

    After the branch is pushed and everything is ready, present a short **readiness summary**:
    - Release branch name and remote URL (or `git log origin/production..origin/release-<version> --oneline`)
    - Final PR title and full body (including checklists and Deployment Notes)
    - Selected reviewers and merge destination (`production`)

    Then use **`AskQuestion`** for **final approval** before **`gh pr create`**. Options must make clear the user can take time for final checks (e.g. **Approve — create PR now** / **Edit — need changes** / **Wait — not yet**).

    - **Do not** run `gh pr create` until the user explicitly approves in this step.
    - If the user chooses **Edit** or **Wait**, stop and wait — do not create the PR.
    - Only **Approve — create PR now** (or equivalent explicit yes) allows `gh pr create`.

13. **Create PR on remote (only after final approval):**

    ```bash
    gh pr create --base production --head release-<version> --assignee @me --title "..." --body "..."
    gh pr edit <pr-number-or-url> --add-reviewer <reviewer1,reviewer2,...>
    ```

14. **Restore workspace (mandatory)** — see [Stash restore (mandatory)](#stash-restore-mandatory): return to the previous branch, then **pop every stash you created in this release run** (newest first if more than one). Confirm `git stash list` no longer contains those entries and tell the user local changes are restored.

